<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kwara State</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kwara State is a state in western central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, created in 1967, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilorin">Ilorin</a>. It lies where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> south-west meets the north, and much of it was once ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilorin-emirate">Ilorin Emirate</a>, founded in the 1820s as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Ilorin remains its leading traditional ruler.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kwara-state.jpg" alt="Kwara State" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Himalayan Explorer based on work by Uwe Dedering · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nigeria_Kwara_State_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kwara State lies in north central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kogi-state">Kogi State</a> to the east, Niger State to the north, mostly across the Niger River and Lake Jebba, and Ekiti, Osun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> states to the south. Its western border forms part of the international border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>. The state is split between the West Sudanian savanna in the east and the Guinean forest–savanna mosaic in the rest. In the far north-west lies the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgu">Borgu</a> section of the Kainji National Park, and a small part of the Old Oyo National Park lies in the far south-west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the pre-colonial period most of the area was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>. Parts of the west belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgu">Borgu</a> kingdoms, peopled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bariba">Bariba</a>, Boko and Bissa, and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe Kingdom</a>, which existed from 1531 to 1835. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulani-war">Fulani jihad</a> later annexed part of the area and placed it under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwandu-emirate">Gwandu</a> sphere of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. British expeditions occupied the area in the 1890s and 1900s and made it part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-nigeria-protectorate">Northern Nigeria Protectorate</a>, which merged into British Nigeria in 1914.</p><p>After independence in 1960 the area was part of the Northern Region. On 27 May 1967 the military government of General Yakubu Gowon divided the four regions of the federation into twelve states, and Kwara was created from the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilorin">Ilorin</a> and Kabba provinces. It was first named the West Central State and renamed Kwara, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> name for the River Niger, in 1976. It has since lost territory: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idah">Idah</a> and Dekina area went to the new Benue State on 13 February 1976, and on 27 August 1991 five local government areas were cut off to form part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kogi-state">Kogi State</a> while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgu">Borgu</a> was merged with Niger State.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilorin">Ilorin</a>, and the state has sixteen local government areas. Principal groups include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bariba">Bariba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and traditional religions all have followers. Agriculture is the main source of the economy, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, cocoa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, kola nut and palm produce among the cash crops. Attractions include the Esie Museum, the Owu waterfalls and Sobi Hill, the largest landform in Ilorin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kwara State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its emir still reigns in <b>Kwara State</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ilorin-emirate">Ilorin Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
